## 2. The new object

The secret subset is carried as **one string** — ciphertext when encrypted, stringified JSON when
not — so there are no branch-specific field *types*, which means **no discriminated union**: the
object is one flat interface. Two principles still hold: **every public field is signed** (no
unsigned public field — it would be tamperable), and **`secret` is stored but not signed** (so
re-encryption keeps the signature valid).

```ts
// shared/src/rendering/types.ts  (canonical, single source)

type DidEthr = `did:ethr:0x${string}`;   // canonical author form, e.g. "did:ethr:0x1234…"

// the PUBLIC, queryable, SIGNED metadata (all of it is signed)
interface S2SPublicContent {
  specVersion: string;        // envelope/format version, e.g. "1" — pins canonicalization + algos (§2a)
  author: DidEthr;            // was: bare 0x address
  postType: string;           // S2S kind (post/page); NOT how to parse the body
  mediaType: string;          // how to parse the body, e.g. "text/markdown" | "text/html"
  parent: string;
  position: string;
  publication: string;
  locale: string;
  createdAt: number;          // unix seconds; this version's timestamp (last-modified = latest version's, from the log)
  base?: string;
  tags: string[];                       // public + queryable → MUST be signed
  attributes?: Record<string, unknown>; // public + queryable → MUST be signed (no `any` in a signed object)
  plaintextHash: string;      // keccak256 of the secret JSON string (computed before encryption)
}

interface S2SProof {
  type: "eip712";
  signer: `0x${string}`;      // recovered address; MUST equal the EOA in public.author
  signature: `0x${string}`;   // author EOA signature over the EIP-712 digest of S2SPublicContent
}

// the stored object — one flat shape, no union
interface S2SContentItem {
  public: S2SPublicContent;   // signed
  objectId: string;           // = EIP-712 digest of `public` (available pre-upload, pre-CID)
  proof: S2SProof;
  encryption: boolean;        // true → `secret` is ciphertext; false → `secret` is stringified JSON
  secret: string;             // ciphertext OR JSON.stringify({ title, slug, content, custom })
}
```

- **The secret subset** `{ title, slug, content, custom }` is serialized to one JSON string (`custom`
  typed `Record<string, unknown>`, not `any`). Plain post: that string is `secret`. Encrypted post:
  Lit-encrypt that string → `secret` is the ciphertext. `encryption` says which.
- **No discriminated union, no `S2SEncryptedBlob`.** Both readings of `secret` are `string`; the old
  `dataToEncryptHash` was a previous-Lit-version artifact and is gone. This retires the literal-union /
  zod-`discriminatedUnion` machinery we'd planned — a flat interface is enough now.
- **`secret` is not signed.** `plaintextHash` commits the plaintext instead, so re-encrypting (new
  key/provider) yields different `secret` bytes but the same signature still verifies.
- **`tags` / `attributes`** live in the signed `public` part. **`title`, `slug`, `content`, `custom`**
  live in the secret JSON — so a gated post's URL doesn't leak its title. Public addressing is the
  `objectId` / genesis handle; the renderer (a decryptor) reads `slug` at render time to place output.
- **`isBasedOn` removed.** Edit history is the contract append log (§4). Cross-document derivation is a
  separate, not-yet-built feature; reintroduce a derivation field deliberately when that lands.
- `id: string // Lens post ID` **removed** → `objectId` (this version) + genesis handle (the document,
  in the log §4). `modifiedDate` **dropped** — derived from the log.
- **Canonical JSON:** serialize with **JCS (RFC 8785)** wherever you hash (the secret JSON; the
  `tags`/`attributes` digest inputs), or `plaintextHash`/`objectId` won't reproduce on verify. Pinned
  by `specVersion` — see §2a.

### EIP-712 types (what gets signed)

```ts
const S2S_DOMAIN = { name: "S2S Content", version: "1", chainId: /* Base */ 8453 };

// signs the whole S2SPublicContent. Scalars inline; arrays/objects committed by keccak hash.
const S2S_TYPES = {
  S2SContent: [
    { name: "specVersion",   type: "string"  },  // "1"
    { name: "author",        type: "string"  },  // did:ethr:0x…
    { name: "plaintextHash", type: "bytes32" },   // = keccak of canonical secret JSON
    { name: "postType",      type: "string"  },
    { name: "mediaType",     type: "string"  },
    { name: "parent",        type: "string"  },
    { name: "position",      type: "string"  },
    { name: "publication",   type: "string"  },
    { name: "locale",        type: "string"  },
    { name: "createdAt",     type: "uint256" },   // unix seconds (matches the interface `number`)
    { name: "base",          type: "string"  },   // "" if absent
    { name: "tagsHash",      type: "bytes32" },   // keccak256 of canonical(tags)
    { name: "attributesHash",type: "bytes32" },   // keccak256 of canonical(attributes), 0x00… if none
  ],
};
```

Array/object public fields (`tags`, `attributes`) are committed by hash so the typed struct stays
flat — but they ARE signed, so they can't be tampered with after issuance.

EIP-712 is chosen over a raw sig so the same digest is verifiable on-chain (EIP-1271 / Safe path) if
ever needed, and `recover` gives the signer directly.

### 2a. What `specVersion` pins (write these in the spec, not as object fields)

`specVersion: "1"` fixes the rules a verifier must apply, so they can evolve without ambiguity:

- **Canonicalization:** **JCS (RFC 8785)** for every JSON that gets hashed — the secret JSON, and the
  `tags`/`attributes` inputs to their keccak digests. This is the top footgun: if signer and verifier
  serialize keys/whitespace differently, every signature fails. "Deterministic stringify" is not
  enough; name JCS.
- **Hash:** keccak256. **Signature:** EIP-712 typed data, domain above.
- A future `"2"` may change any of these; the version field is what lets a verifier pick the right set.

