## 3. The signing method (new)

```ts
// shared — new module, e.g. shared/src/identity/sign-content.ts

async function buildSignedContentItem(
  pub: Omit<S2SPublicContent, "author" | "plaintextHash">,
  body: { title: string; slug: string; content: string; custom?: Record<string, unknown> },
  signer,                       // author EOA
  encryption: boolean,
): Promise<S2SContentItem> {
  const json          = jcs(body);                     // RFC 8785 canonical JSON (specVersion "1")
  const plaintextHash = keccak256(json);               // computed BEFORE encryption

  const publicPart: S2SPublicContent = { ...pub, author: toDidEthr(signer.address), plaintextHash };

  const objectId  = eip712Digest(S2S_DOMAIN, S2S_TYPES, publicPart);   // persistent version id, pre-CID
  const signature = await signer.signTypedData(S2S_DOMAIN, S2S_TYPES, publicPart);
  const proof: S2SProof = { type: "eip712", signer: signer.address, signature };

  const secret = encryption ? await lit.encrypt(json) : json;   // one string either way

  return { public: publicPart, objectId, proof, encryption, secret };
}
```

`plaintextHash` is over the exact `json` string; encryption (if any) wraps that same string. `secret`
holds the result; it is never part of the signed payload.

Order is load-bearing: **hash → digest(=objectId) → sign → encrypt → upload(→CID) → contract append.**
The objectId exists at sign time; the CID does not, which is exactly why the CID can't be the id.

---

## 4. Contract — append log

The contract stores, per document, an **append-only** ordered list of versions.

```
append(genesis, objectId, cid, author, proof)   // genesis == objectId for the first entry
```

- **Genesis entry** establishes the document's persistent identity and its authorizing
  `did:ethr` author.
- **Every later append re-points the document** to a new (objectId, CID) — a new signed version.
- **Latest** = last non-tombstone entry.
- **Retraction** = append a tombstone version (append-only; never delete).

### NEW INVARIANT (must be enforced or the log is forgeable)

> **INV-append:** the contract MUST gate every `append` to the genesis author (or permitted
> publication owners), verified against the same `did:ethr` identity recorded in the genesis entry.
> This is the existing author/publication gate, extended to guard appends, not only the initial
> publish.

Each version is independently self-verifying (it carries its own author EOA signature over its own
payload). The contract adds ordering, "which is latest," and a **public, decryption-free** answer to
"current version of document X, authored by whom."

---

## 5. Verification — who checks what, where

| Layer | Verifier | Needs decrypt? | Proves |
|---|---|---|---|
| On-chain record (append log) | anyone, incl. readers | **no** | who published which objectId/CID, gated to which author |
| Author EOA sig over plaintext hash | decryptors: author / publication owners / **renderer** | yes | who authored the content |
| PKP sig over rendered HTML | anyone | no | the protocol rendered this, gated; carries authorship transitively |

Two methods to add:

```ts
// renderer-side (a permitted decryptor): run before rendering
async function verifyAuthorship(item: S2SContentItem): Promise<boolean> {
  const json = item.encryption ? await lit.decrypt(item.secret) : item.secret;   // canonical JSON string
  if (keccak256(json) !== item.public.plaintextHash) return false;

  const recovered = recoverTypedData(S2S_DOMAIN, S2S_TYPES, item.public, item.proof.signature);
  return recovered === eoaOf(item.public.author) && recovered === item.proof.signer;
}

// public-side: no decryption — read the log
function resolveLatest(genesis): { objectId, cid, author } { /* read append log */ }
```

Do **not** also sign the ciphertext to chase public verifiability — redundant with the record and
binds the signature to one specific encryption. The record is the public layer.

