## 6. Open decisions (flag, don't guess)

1. **DAG-assembly removal:** confirm the append-log-as-index supersedes the single-growing-DAG, then
   the per-publication DAG re-upload code can be retired.
2. **Cross-document derivation (deferred):** the dropped `isBasedOn` was the only thing covering
   "based on *another author's* work" (the log only covers your own versions). Reintroduce a
   derivation field when that feature is actually built — not now.
**Resolved (license):** **not in the object.** The permission model is the access gate — `encryption`
on/off plus the on-chain decrypt conditions — which already answers "who may use this." A declarative
legal license would be unenforced text sitting beside an enforced mechanism (and could contradict it,
e.g. "CC-BY" on a gated post). Access control (who can *obtain*) and usage rights (what they may *do*
after) are different axes; only the former is mechanism here. The January "permitted license" pillar
is effectively already implemented *by* the access gate. If a declarative reuse-license is ever wanted
for public content, add it as an explicitly-non-enforced, publication-level annotation — not a signed
per-item field. Bilateral deal/revenue terms remain on-chain (grant/accept + license txs) regardless.

**Resolved:** `slug` lives in the secret JSON (with `title`/`content`/`custom`), not the public part —
so a gated post's URL can't leak its title; public addressing is `objectId` / genesis handle, and the
renderer reads `slug` after decryption to place output.

